---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      TDEE is the total number of calories your body burns in a day. It is your Basal Metabolic Rate,
      the energy you use at complete rest, multiplied by an activity factor between 1.2 and 1.9. This
      calculator estimates BMR with the Mifflin-St Jeor equation from your sex, age, height, and
      weight, scales it by your activity level, and then gives you concrete calorie targets: eat below
      the maintenance number to lose weight, above it to gain. About 7,700 calories equals a kilogram,
      so a 550 calorie daily gap moves roughly half a kilogram a week.
      This daily calorie calculator works as a maintenance calorie calculator and an energy expenditure calculator:
      it shows your maintenance calories, then a calorie deficit or a calorie surplus, scaled by the activity multiplier you pick.
      Sanity-checking a calorie target suggested by a fitness app is a good use of the number.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try The TDEE Calculator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How TDEE Works <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Your daily burn comes in two parts. The first and largest is BMR, the energy your body spends
      just staying alive: keeping your heart beating, your lungs working, and your temperature steady.
      For most people that is 60 to 70 percent of the total. The second part is everything you do on
      top of that, from walking and typing to formal exercise. TDEE combines the two by taking your
      BMR and multiplying it by a single activity factor, a shortcut that captures how much your
      movement adds to the resting figure. A sedentary person uses a factor of 1.2, while someone
      training hard most days uses up to 1.9. To choose the right activity multiplier, weigh BMR versus
      TDEE honestly: the resting figure is fixed, but the multiplier is where most people overestimate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="bmr-formula">
    <h2>The Mifflin-St Jeor Formula <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      BMR here is estimated with the Mifflin-St Jeor equation, considered one of the most accurate for
      the general population. For men it is 10 times weight in kilograms, plus 6.25 times height in
      centimetres, minus 5 times age in years, plus 5. For women the final term is minus 161 instead
      of plus 5. A 30-year-old man of 70 kg and 175 cm has a BMR of about 1,649 calories, and at a
      moderate activity level his TDEE is roughly 2,556. If you enter imperial units, the calculator
      converts pounds and inches first, so the result is the same either way.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="calorie-goals">
    <h2>Turning TDEE Into a Calorie Goal <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Weight change is mostly about the gap between what you eat and what you burn. Because a kilogram
      of body weight holds about 7,700 calories, a daily deficit of 550 calories predicts around half
      a kilogram of loss a week, and the same surplus predicts a similar gain. The tool lays out these
      targets for you: a gentle loss, a steady loss, maintenance, and a gain. It floors any loss target
      at your BMR, because eating below your resting requirement for long is neither comfortable nor
      wise.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Estimate first, then measure">
      <p>A formula cannot know your exact metabolism. A calorie deficit versus surplus is the whole game, but TDEE is an estimate to test against the scale, not an exact measurement. Track your weight for two to three weeks and adjust. The scale is the real calculator.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="examples">
    <h2>Examples <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p><strong>Man, 30, 70 kg, 175 cm, moderate.</strong> For example, BMR is about 1,649 and, at an activity multiplier of 1.55, TDEE is roughly 2,556 maintenance calories.</p>
    <p><strong>Aiming to lose.</strong> For example, a 550 calorie deficit sets the target near 2,006, predicting about half a kilogram off per week.</p>
    <p><strong>Aiming to gain.</strong> For example, a 300 calorie surplus lifts the target to about 2,856, a slow steady gain that favours muscle over fat.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Overstating activity level">
      <p>
        The activity factor has a big effect: jumping from moderate to very active can add several
        hundred calories to the target. Most people move less than they think outside of workouts,
        so when in doubt pick the lower level and let real results correct it.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Cutting below BMR">
      <p>
        Slashing calories under your BMR to lose faster tends to backfire: it is hard to sustain,
        costs muscle, and leads to rebound eating. A moderate deficit you can hold for months
        beats an aggressive one you abandon in a week.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="Give it time">
      <p>Daily weight swings are mostly water and food in transit. Judge a calorie target by its two to three week trend, not by tomorrow's reading. Everything here runs in your browser and never leaves your device.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
